    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S4138A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the tax law, in relation to the\ncreation of a biotechnology research and development investment tax\ncredit\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo incentivize the growth of the Bio/Med industry within New York\nState by providing a tax credit for Bio/Med research and development\nactivities conducted within the state.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nAdds a new section to the Tax Law to create a tax credit for Bio/Med\nresearch and development activities occurring within the state for\ncalendar years 2017 through 2020. The credit is capped annually at $50\nmillion. An enhanced credit is provided for activities occurring in\nupstate counties. The bill also adds conforming subsections to\nsections 210 and 606 of the Tax Law.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nThe Tax Law includes a limited tax credit for investment activities.\nAdditionally, the Tax Law includes tax credits for certain economic\nactivity including brownfield remediation, film production and biofuel\nproduction.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe Bio/Med industry is revolutionizing health care allowing people to\nlive healthier and longer lives. Advances in science, engineering and\ngenetics are rapidly being translated into medicines, precise and\ninnovative diagnostic tests, devices and equipment. The field is also\nrapidly expanding leading to significant increased economic activity\nand job growth.\n\nMany states have sought to capitalize on the growth of the industry by\nestablishing targeted programs and incentives to attract investment\nand expansion from Bio/Med companies. These efforts include tax\ncredits, grants and specialized educational and training programs.\nThe programs have proved successful with those states with such\nprograms seeing significantly greater growth than states without.\n\nWhile the Bio/Med industry has a significant presence in New York\n(among the top 10 states in the size of its industry and scale and\nreach of its research and ranked 8th in medical technology\nspecifically among states), the state has not sought to take advantage\nof its strategic assets including higher educational institutions,\nhighly skilled employees and existing Bio/Med companies (in Upstate\nNew York alone the industry contributes 28,000 high-skilled jobs) to\ngrow the industry within the state. As a result, New York has fallen\nfurther behind leading states.\n\nA Bio/Med research and investment tax credit will reposition New York\nas an attractive state for Bio/Med investment. Research & development\ntax credits are proven to spur economic activity. Studies have found\nthat research and development tax credits increase private research\nspending, employment and wages. 70% of the funds from these tax\ncredits go towards labor costs and support high-tech jobs. A targeted\nBiolMed research and development investment tax credit will attract\ninvestment from the rapidly expanding and promising field.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015 - A.4138/A.7045 - INVESTIGATIONS/Ways & Means\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nThe credit is capped at $50 million per year through 2019 which will\nspur economic activity and job growth which will significantly offset,\nif not completely offset any loss of revenue to the state.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill shall take effect immediately.\n",
